I *used* to have the problem.  I've got a '286 with 1.5Meg.

The problem is that Nethack detects how much memory you have, and swaps stuff
to disk *by itself*.

I found the cure: give it as much memory as possible.

No caches, no ramdisk, no deletion-tracking software, and almost no TSRs.
I can probably start re-introducing TSRs, but why bother, I'm having fun...

Bingo!  Instant speedup to acceptable levels.

(Actually, the problem occurred when I went from DOS 5 to 6, because of the
extra memory-using junk DOS 6 puts in automatically.)

One word of warning: using '!' to shell to dos causes it to slow down again
after you get back.  Saving and restoring fixes that.


